  "image_name": "2ec3023dc1f9716d92709a5df427322f.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how tissue-resident macrophages, synovial fibroblast subsets, endothelial cells, and infiltrating immune cells interact within inflamed synovium to drive tissue damage. The central idea is a reciprocal signaling network rather than a one-way pathway.\n\nTissue-resident macrophages release inflammatory mediators such as TNF, IL-1β, IL-6, PGE2, and GM-CSF that act on synovial fibroblasts, while also contributing directly to tissue damage through factors including MMPs and TGFβ-related signals. Synovial fibroblast subsets sit at the center of the diagram and appear to amplify inflammation by secreting chemokines, IL-6, VEGF, IFNβ, MMPs, and TGFβ. These outputs recruit and influence immune cells in the synovium, promote matrix-degrading activity, and support vascular changes.\n\nAnother major feature is crosstalk with blood vessels. Both macrophages and fibroblasts signal to the endothelium, and VEGF, IL-6, and TGFβ are highlighted as key mediators of endothelial activation. Activated endothelium then appears to facilitate immune-cell entry into the synovium.\n\nImmune cells within the synovium feed back onto fibroblasts through TNF, IL-1β, and IFNγ, while fibroblasts return signals such as IL-6, PGE2, and GM-CSF. Overall, the figure depicts a self-reinforcing inflammatory circuit linking stromal, vascular, and immune compartments to synovial injury.",
